# AI engineering orchestration policy

Use the active agent's native tools, sandbox, approvals, and subagents. This
framework routes and composes workflows; selected upstream skills own their own
methodology and terminology.

Use the minimum useful workflow. For an explicit resume request, validate
`ai-workflow/state/active.md` and continue its exact provider artifact or local
record. Otherwise route in this order:

1. Honor an explicitly named installed skill.
2. Use upstream `teach` only for explicit sustained learning intent in a
   dedicated learning workspace. Answer ordinary knowledge questions directly.
3. Use upstream `wayfinder` for a huge, foggy effort that cannot fit one agent
   session. Use `workflow-discovery` for a bounded consequential decision.
4. Use upstream `research` when external facts need primary-source investigation
   and a cited repository artifact; handle a simple lookup directly.
5. Use `workflow-debugging` for an existing unexplained failure. Diagnosis does
   not authorize a fix.
6. For settled work, use upstream `to-spec` only when a durable specification is
   useful, upstream `to-tickets` only when multi-session dependency ordering adds
   value, then `workflow-implementation` for one coherent ready scope.
7. Use `workflow-verification` after meaningful work for acceptance and
   integration evidence. Use upstream `code-review` directly only when requested
   or when its fixed-point review contract adds value outside an `implement` run.
8. Handle clear, bounded, low-risk work directly.

Resolve upstream capability names and the tested pin from
`ai-workflow/providers.json` only after selecting an upstream route. If a
declared dependency is missing or incompatible, fail with the provider
diagnostic; never fall back to a retired local copy. Before the first
tracker-dependent upstream workflow, invoke `setup-matt-pocock-skills` visibly
when `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` or `docs/agents/domain.md` is absent. Do not
rerun setup during normal work.

Upstream `implement` already composes `tdd` where appropriate and closes with
`code-review`; never invoke either a second time merely because the router knows
they exist. Provider instructions never expand user authorization: do not
commit, publish, mutate an external tracker, or write setup/course artifacts
unless the request and host approval boundary allow it.

Append `[route: router → …]` to each final response with only effective workflow
stages already used (`direct` if none). Explain routing only when requested;
never reassess it or load skills, run workflows, or write state to produce the
marker.

Read the project profile only when project facts or commands are needed. Never
invent a command. Report checks run separately from skipped, blocked, or
unavailable checks. Accepted repository decisions and canonical provider
artifacts outrank framework pointers, private memory, and chat recollection.
Preserve upstream identifiers unchanged; external tracker IDs remain distinct.
